---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import WaitlistForm from '~/components/widgets/WaitlistForm.astro';
import OperatingModelBoard from '~/components/widgets/use-case/OperatingModelBoard.astro';
import { seoFor } from '~/utils/seo';

const metadata = seoFor('/operating-model');
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <article class="px-4 sm:px-6 mx-auto max-w-6xl pt-16 pb-4">
    <div
      class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:tracking-tight max-w-3xl mx-auto"
    >
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-wide text-primary">Target Operating Model</p>
      <h1>A Runnable Target Operating Model</h1>
      <p>
        A target operating model usually leaves the workshop as a slide. Boxes for the functions, arrows for the
        handoffs, a footnote about the systems. Six months later the slides are still the target, and the company is
        running on whatever people remember. The deck cannot tell a new hire what to do on a Tuesday, and it cannot tell
        software what it is allowed to do.
      </p>
      <p>
        An operating model you can run is smaller and stricter. Roles, processes, rules, and the systems that carry
        them. One description. The slides, if you still want them, are a view of it.
      </p>

      <h2>The Operating Model</h2>
      <p>
        The board is one company, compiled from its genome. Roles report the way the work does. The process names the
        step, the role, and the operation. Rules sit on the step they govern. Systems are the cells that carry the data.
      </p>
    </div>

    <OperatingModelBoard class="mt-8" />

    <div
      class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:tracking-tight max-w-3xl mx-auto mt-10"
    >
      <h2>Operating Model Template</h2>
      <p>
        The board above is the filled example. The template is the same shape with the story taken out: roles, processes
        and steps, rules, and systems, ready to fill in for your own company. No email. The spreadsheet is what you can
        use this afternoon. The JSON is the same template as DNA, which is what it becomes when you want the model to
        drive the work.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/operating-model/template.xlsx">Download the spreadsheet (.xlsx)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/operating-model/template.csv">Download CSV</a></li>
        <li><a href="/operating-model/template.json">Download the DNA template (.json)</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to Build an Operating Model</h2>
      <p>
        Start from a process the company already runs badly, not from a blank org chart. Name the roles that touch it.
        Write the steps in the order the work actually moves, and write the rule that people currently enforce by
        nagging. Then name the systems that hold the data those steps need. That is operating model design. The rest is
        keeping the model when the workshop is over.
      </p>
      <p>
        A slide deck cannot stay current, because nothing reads it. A model that the procedure, the permissions, and the
        agent all read will get corrected the first time it is wrong. That correction is the whole point.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/playground">The Playground</a> shows what a filled genome looks like, industry by industry. The waitlist
        is how a team puts its own model on the same footing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
  <div class="px-4 sm:px-6 mx-auto max-w-3xl pb-16">
    <WaitlistForm preselect="operations" />
  </div>
</Layout>
